      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 3 of 7 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 (water meters — R49 is for water meters for cold potable water and hot water).\n\nThe fragment contains:\n1. Certificate number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\n2. Manufacturer: Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\n3. Model number: MS2500 / MV110\n4. Type details table with:\n   - Nominal diameter: 50, 65, 80 (three DN variants)\n   - Q1 values: 0.252, 0.158, 0.400, 0.250, 0.640, 0.400 — six values across... wait, three DNs but 6 columns?\n\nLet me look at the table structure carefully:\n\n```\n<tr><td><i>Nominal diameter:</i></td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\"><i>50</i></td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\"><i>65</i></td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\"><i>80</i></td></tr>\n```\n\nSo \"Nominal diameter\" row has 50 spanning 2 columns, 65 spanning 2 columns, 80 spanning 2 columns. That means each DN has 2 sub-columns.\n\nThen:\n- Q1: 0.252, 0.158, 0.400, 0.250, 0.640, 0.400 — six values\n- Q2: 0.403, 0.252, 0.640, 0.400, 1.024, 0.640\n- Q3: 63.0, 63.0, 100, 100, 160, 160\n- Q4: 73.0, 73.0, 125, 125, 200, 200\n- Q3/Q1: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 (all)\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25 (all)\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic water meter\n- Accuracy class: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2\n- Pressure loss class: Δp40, Δp40, Δp40 (per DN)\n- Indicating range [m³]: 99 999, 999 999, 999 999 (per DN)\n- Resolution of the indicating device [m³]: 0.000 1, 0.001, 0.001 (per DN)\n\nSo the structure: for each DN (50, 65, 80), there are two variants based on Q3/Q1 ratio: 250 and 400.\n\nSo the 6 configurations are:\n- DN50, Q3/Q1=250: Q1=0.252, Q2=0.403, Q3=63.0, Q4=73.0, class 1\n- DN50, Q3/Q1=400: Q1=0.158, Q2=0.252, Q3=63.0, Q4=73.0, class 2\n- DN65, Q3/Q1=250: Q1=0.400, Q2=0.640, Q3=100, Q4=125, class 1\n- DN65, Q3/Q1=400: Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q3=100, Q4=125, class 2\n- DN80, Q3/Q1=250: Q1=0.640, Q2=1.024, Q3=160, Q4=200, class 1\n- DN80, Q3/Q1=400: Q1=0.400, Q2=0.640, Q3=160, Q4=200, class 2\n\nInteresting — accuracy class 1 corresponds to Q3/Q1=250 and class 2 to Q3/Q1=400. That makes sense: higher Q3/Q1 ratio → class 2 (harder to achieve class 1).\n\nWait — but for water meters, class 1 and 2 relate to accuracy. R49 allows either. Q3/Q1=250 can be class 1 or 2, and Q3/Q1=400 is class 2. Actually the accuracy class row says: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2 — so the 250 variants are class 1 and 400 variants are class 2.\n\nAlso from power supply table on next page:\n- Module designation for all nominal diameters: MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV110 LV, MS2500 / MV110 LLV\n- Type: HV = 100–240 V AC; LV = 24–36 V AC/DC; LLV = 10–48 V DC\n- Frequency: HV = 45–66 Hz; LV = 45–66 Hz; LLV = -\n- Internal backup source: HV = 3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery; LV same; LLV = -\n\nSo there are configuration axes:\n1. Nominal diameter × Q3/Q1 ratio (6 hydraulic variants)\n2. Power supply module (HV, LV, LLV — 3 variants)\n\nModel family: The model number is \"MS2500 / MV110\". Hmm — is the whole thing one model or a family? The certificate covers 6 hydraulic variants × 3 power supply modules. I'd model this as model_family with family name \"MS2500 / MV110\" and models as the 6 hydraulic variants (DN + ratio), plus config_level for power supply module.\n\nActually, the power supply module is a configuration axis: \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" — MS2500/MV110 HV, LV, LLV. So power supply attributes vary by this axis → config_level.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\"\n}\n```\n\nThe scheme: The number format \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" — \"A\" appears after the year. So scheme = \"A\". Hmm, should I infer this? The certificate number contains \"-A-\" which indicates scheme A. This is reasonable inference from the number itself. I'll include it since it's derivable from the number. Actually, let me be careful — the instruction says \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The scheme letter A is literally part of the certificate number, so it's present. I'll include scheme: \"A\".\n\nissuing_authority: The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut\" — that's the Czech Metrology Institute (CMI). It's visible in the stamp. Should I include it? The fragment only shows the stamp text. The oiml_issuer_id is not present. I could include the name from the stamp. Hmm, it's partly visible. I think it's safe to include name \"Český metrologický institut\" since the certificate number \"CZ1\" also hints at Czech Republic. But to be conservative, I'll include it as it's explicitly in the markdown. Address lines are not present. I'll include the name only.\n\napplicants[]: Not explicitly stated in this fragment; only \"Manufacturer:\" is labeled. Per rule 12, some certs label Manufacturer only. So manufacturers[]: [{name: \"Hemina SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana\", \"350 44 Italy\"]}]. Actually the address is semicolon separated: \"Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\". Split into address_lines.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (measuring principle: Electromagnetic water meter; R49 is water meters). The category examples include \"Measuring system\", \"Gas meter\" etc. \"Water meter\" is appropriate.\n- type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"] — model number. Also the modules \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\". Should those go in type_designations? They're module designations of the same instrument. I'll put \"MS2500 / MV110\" as the type designation and mention HV/LV/LLV in the config axis / model variants.\n- module_designation: For R49, module designation isn't the R60-style \"Analog load cell\". The table says \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter: MS2500 / MV110 HV|LV|LLV\". Hmm, this refers to the whole instrument's module variants by power supply. I could set module_designation: null or \"MS2500 / MV110 (HV, LV, LLV power supply modules)\". Actually maybe I should record it via components or config axis. I'll record config-level.\n\nActually wait — is the MS2500/MV110 a \"module\"? The table row header \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" suggests each power supply variant is a module designation. In R49-1, the certificate application form asks for \"Designation of the module\". For water meters, the whole meter may be the module. So module_designation could be the three variants. I'll set module_designation: null and instead capture it in the config axis. Hmm, or I could list \"MS2500/MV110\" since the power supply table identifies the modules. Let me set module_designation to null and capture the HV/LV/LLV in config_level.\n\nActually let me reconsider: certified_type.module_designation per the schema examples is \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null — for R60. Here, \"Electromagnetic water meter\" is the measuring principle, not a module. I'll leave module_designation out (omit) or null. I'll omit it to avoid fabrication. Hmm, but the schema shows it as a field. Rules say omit if not present. The table header literally says \"Designation of the module\" though. I think it's reasonable to include: module_designation: \"MS2500 / MV110 (HV / LV / LLV power supply variants)\". Hmm, that's a bit of a stretch. Alternatively, record components? No — the power supply variants are not separate components; they're variants of the same meter.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"MS2500 / MV110\"\n- models: 6 hydraulic variants:\n  - {\"model_id\": \"DN50-Q3/Q1=250\", \"label\": \"DN 50, Q3=63 m³/h, Q3/Q1=250\", \"attributes\": {...}}\n  - etc.\n\nOr model_id like \"DN50/250\"? Let me use model_id: \"DN50 Q3/Q1=250\" etc. Actually per the R60 example style, model_id like \"Emax=25t\". Here maybe \"DN50-Q3=63-Q3Q1=250\". Keep it simpler: \"DN50, Q3/Q1=250\".\n\nCharacteristics model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, accuracy_class, indicating_range, resolution_of_indicating_device, nominal_diameter, pressure_loss_class, q2_over_q1 (constant 1.6 — constant → type_level), q4_over_q3 (constant 1.25 → type_level).\n\nWait: q2_over_q1 = 1.6 for all six; q4_over_q3 = 1.25 for all six → type_level.\n\naccuracy_class varies: 1 for the 250 variants, 2 for the 400 variants → model_level.\n\nnominal_diameter: 50, 65, 80 (each shared by two models) → model_level with values per model.\n\nindicating_range [m³]: per DN (99 999 for DN50; 999 999 for DN65, DN80) → model_level. Note \"99 999\" = 99999 and \"999 999\" = 999999 (space thousands separator).\n\nresolution_of_indicating_device [m³]: 0.000 1 → 0.0001 for DN50; 0.001 for DN65, DN80 → model_level.\n\nresolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing: \"-\" → not a value; omit.\n\npressure_loss_class: Δp40 for all three DNs → type_level with value \"Δp40\"? That's a class designation, string value \"Δp40\". Hmm, the canonical label rule says no LaTeX etc. \"Δp40\" is a class name; keep as string value \"Δp40\". unit null. Or I could put value 40 with unit... no, it's a class designation. Value: \"Δp40\". Actually maybe cleaner: \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp40\"}. Since it's the same for all, type_level. Note there's a subscript-ish formatting (Δ<i>p</i>40). Keep \"Δp40\".\n\nwater_pressure_class: MAP16 → type_level, value \"MAP16\".\n\nreverse_flow: \"designed to measure\" → type_level string.\n\nenvironmental_class: \"O (-25 to +55 °C)\" → This has a numeric range embedded. Could split: environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}, plus operating_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55, unit \"°C\"}. That seems good. Or keep as one string. I'll do environmental_class: \"O\" and temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55, unit °C}. Hmm — the class O per R49-2 means -25/+55 °C. Extract both.\n\nelectromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\" → string value \"E1, E2\" or list. The value shape allows number|string. I'll use \"E1, E2\". Could also be [\"E1\",\"E2\"] but schema says value: number|{min,max}|string. Use \"E1, E2\". Hmm, maybe \"E1,E2\" — keep with space.\n\nmechanical_class: \"B, O\" → string \"B, O\".\n\ntemperature_class: \"For liner PTFE - T90; T50; T30; For liner Rilsan - T50; T30 from DN50; For liner Ebonite - T50; T30 from DN200; For liner polypropylene - T50; T30\" → string value. Keep as a single descriptive string. Could be config_level by liner material, but the \"from DN50\"/\"from DN200\" qualifiers make it messy. I'll keep as type_level string.\n\nmaximum_admissible_temperature: \"For liners PTFE - 90°C; For liner Polypropylen, Rilsan and Ebonite - 50°C\" → config_level by liner? Or a string. Could do config_level:\n- attribute: maximum_admissible_temperature, axis: liner_material, values: [{condition: \"PTFE\", value: 90, unit °C}, {condition: \"Polypropylene, Rilsan, Ebonite\", value: 50, unit °C}]\nThat's a nice use of config_level. Similarly temperature_class by liner:\n- attribute: temperature_class, axis: liner_material, values: [{condition: \"PTFE\", value: \"T90, T50, T30\"}, {condition: \"Rilsan\", value: \"T50, T30 (from DN50)\"}, {condition: \"Ebonite\", value: \"T50, T30 (from DN200)\"}, {condition: \"Polypropylene\", value: \"T50, T30\"}]\n\nThat's reasonable. Alternatively keep raw strings in type_level. I think config_level is more informative and matches rule 5. Let me do that for both temperature_class and maximum_admissible_temperature.\n\nmaximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa → type_level {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}.\n\norientation_limitation: \"any\" → type_level string.\n\nmaximum_permissible_error_lower_zone (MPEl): \"±3 % (Class 1); ±5 % (Class 2)\" → config_level by accuracy_class:\n- attribute: mpe_lower_flowrate_zone, axis: accuracy_class, values: [{condition: \"Class 1\", value: 3, unit \"%\"}, {condition: \"Class 2\", value: 5, unit \"%\"}]. Sign ± can't be captured in number; maybe value \"±3\" string? Rule says numbers not strings. ±3% as value 3 with... hmm. I could record value: {min: -3, max: 3}? That's overinterpretation. Better: value \"±3%\" as string? The rules prefer numbers. MPE is expressed as ±3 %. I think value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\", and the ± implied. But losing the ± is slightly lossy. Alternatively value: \"±3 %\". Hmm.\n\nGiven \"Structured values — STRICT\", I'll use numeric: value 3 with unit \"%\" and attribute name maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone — the ± is inherent in MPE. Actually, to be safe and informative, I could use value {min: -3, max: 3}? No — MPE ±3% means the error must be within ±3%, i.e., range [-3, +3] %. That's actually a correct interpretation! {min: -3, max: 3}, unit \"%\". Hmm, that's a defensible reading. But it might confuse. I'll go with value \"±3\" as string? The rules say \"Numbers, not strings\" for numeric values. But \"±3\" is not parseable as a number. I think {min:-3, max:3} is elegant. Let me consider: for MPE values in matrix tables, the R137 example uses numbers. For MPEs, I'll use value: \"±3%\" — no...\n\nDecision: use {min: -3, max: 3}, unit \"%\" for ±3%. It captures the meaning precisely. Similarly for upper zone:\n- ≤30°C water: ±1% class 1, ±2% class 2\n- >30°C water: ±2% class 1, ±3% class 2\n\nMPEu varies by BOTH accuracy_class AND water temperature → config_level with two axes? The schema allows config_level[] entries each with one axis. I could emit two entries:\n1. attribute: maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrate_zone, axis: accuracy_class × water_temperature... axis is a single string; I can make axis: \"water_temperature\", and conditions \"≤ 30 °C, Class 1\" etc. Or axis: \"accuracy_class and water temperature\" with compound conditions.\n\nSimplest: axis: \"water_temperature\", values: [\n {condition: \"≤ 30 °C, Class 1\", value: {min:-1, max:1}, unit \"%\"},\n {condition: \"≤ 30 °C, Class 2\", value: {min:-2, max:2}, unit \"%\"},\n {condition: \"> 30 °C, Class 1\", value: {min:-2, max:2}, unit \"%\"},\n {condition: \"> 30 °C, Class 2\", value: {min:-3, max:3}, unit \"%\"}\n]\n\nThat works.\n\nAlso MPEl by accuracy_class:\naxis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"Class 1\", value: {min:-3, max:3}, unit: \"%\"}, {condition: \"Class 2\", value: {min:-5, max:5}, unit: \"%\"}]\n\ncut_off: \"0,2%\" → European decimal! Convert to 0.2, unit \"%\". Note \"Cut-off\" row: 0,2% → value 0.2, unit_symbol \"%\".\n\ndetection_empty_pipe: \"on\" → string \"on\".\n\nsoftware_version: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\" → string.\n\ncrc_checksum: \"9BA34F3B\" → string.\n\nEUT testing requirements:\n- category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- case: \"Case D\" → could go to type_level: eut_test_case: {value: \"D\"} or \"Case D\". I'll record eut_category and eut_case.\n\nInstallation details:\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\" (screw thread label but value Flanged)\n- minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: 0 mm → value 0, unit \"mm\"\n- minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\" → string\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\"\n- mounting: \"-\" omit\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- other_relevant_information: \"-\" omit\n- length: 200, 200, 200 [mm] — three values matching the three DNs (columns colspan 3 align with the three DN columns). Actually the length row on page 7 has 4 columns total: label + 3 values (200, 200, 200). The 3 values correspond to DN50, DN65, DN80. So length per DN = 200 mm for all → could be type_level 200 mm since all equal. Yes! All three are 200, so type_level: {value: 200, unit: \"mm\"}. Good simplification. Though hmm, is the length really per DN? Row \"Length [mm]\" with 3 cells each 200. Since all equal, type_level is fine.\n\nElectrical installation details:\n- wiring_instructions: \"-\" omit\n- mounting_arrangement: \"-\" omit\n- orientation_limitations: \"-\" omit\n\nPower supply table (config axis: power supply module — HV/LV/LLV):\n- MS2500/MV110 HV: 100–240 V AC, 45–66 Hz, internal backup 3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh; Battery\n- MS2500/MV110 LV: 24–36 V AC/DC, 45–66 Hz, backup same\n- MS2500/MV110 LLV: 10–48 V DC, no frequency (DC), no backup\n\nRule 3 example: \"110-240V AC 50/60Hz\" split into voltage, frequency, type. So:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute: power_supply_voltage, axis: power_supply_module, values:\n   - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", value: {min:100, max:240}, unit \"V\", plus power_supply_type AC}\n   - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", value: {min:24, max:36}, unit \"V\", type AC/DC}\n   - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", value: {min:10, max:48}, unit \"V\", type DC}\n\nBut config_level value objects: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" per rule 5. Where does power_supply_type go? Rule 3 says separate characteristic power_supply_type. With config axis, I'd need separate config_level entries for type:\n2. attribute: power_supply_type, axis: power_supply_module, values: [{condition HV, value \"AC\"}, {condition LV, value \"AC/DC\"}, {condition LLV, value \"DC\"}]\n3. attribute: power_supply_frequency, axis: power_supply_module, values: [{condition HV, value {min:45,max:66}, unit \"Hz\"}, {condition LV, {min:45,max:66}, \"Hz\"}, {condition LLV, value null}] — LLV has \"-\", omit that value or set null. Since \"-\" means not applicable (DC), omit LLV from frequency values.\n4. attribute: internal_backup_source_voltage, axis: power_supply_module, values: [{condition HV, value 3.7, unit \"V\"}, {condition LV, 3.7, \"V\"}] — note \"3,7V DC\" is European decimal → 3.7. And \"5200mAh\" battery capacity → separate: internal_backup_battery_capacity: [{HV: 5200 mAh}, {LV: 5200 mAh}]. backup type \"Battery\".\n5. minimum_battery_life_time: \"-\" for all → omit.\n\nAlternatively, the internal backup source as one string per module. I'll split into voltage and capacity.\n\nmatrix_tables: Should I emit a matrix table for the main type details table? It's a 6-column (per variant) attribute table. The R137 example shows extracting matrix_tables AND model_level. Here the main table maps attributes × 6 variants. I could emit a matrix_table \"Type details by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\" with rows per variant. That adds redundancy but is per the example pattern. Let me emit one matrix_table with columns: nominal_diameter, q3_over_q1, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4 and 6 rows. Units m³/h. Plus indicating range and resolution per DN — those are per DN not per variant; could add rows duplicated or separate small table. I'll include indicating_range and resolution columns repeated per variant for completeness, or keep matrix table focused on flows. I'll include all: columns [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"indicating_range\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\"], column_units [ \"mm\", null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³\", \"m³\" ].\n\nThe schema for matrix_tables shows: name, columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[]. The R137 example adds \"column_units\". I'll include column_units too since shown in example.\n\nNow model_family models — 6 variants. model_id naming: \"DN50-Q3Q1-250\"? Let me use readable: \"DN50 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN50 Q3/Q1=400\", \"DN65 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN65 Q3/Q1=400\", \"DN80 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN80 Q3/Q1=400\". Order in table: values go 0.252 (DN50-250), 0.158 (DN50-400), 0.400 (DN65-250), 0.250 (DN65-400), 0.640 (DN80-250), 0.400 (DN80-400). Wait — is the column order DN50-250 then DN50-400? The Q3/Q1 row: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400 — yes, alternating. And Q3: 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160 consistent with DN pairs. Accuracy class: 1,2,1,2,1,2 — aligning class 1 with ratio 250 and class 2 with 400.\n\nHmm wait, that's interesting: Q3/Q1 = 250 → class 1, Q3/Q1 = 400 → class 2. But actually for R49, class 1 requires Q3/Q1 ≥ ... it's about accuracy not ratio limits. Fine, take table as-is.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- nominal_diameter (mm): 50/50/65/65/80/80. Unit: nominal diameter DN50 — unit mm. value 50 unit \"mm\". Label \"DN\" maybe. I'll use unit \"mm\".\n- q1 (m³/h): 0.252, 0.158, 0.400, 0.250, 0.640, 0.400\n- q2: 0.403, 0.252, 0.640, 0.400, 1.024, 0.640\n- q3: 63.0, 63.0, 100, 100, 160, 160 → 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160\n- q4: 73, 73, 125, 125, 200, 200\n- q3_over_q1: 250, 400, ... (dimensionless)\n- accuracy_class: \"1\"/\"2\" — numeric 1,2 fine as numbers? Accuracy class 1 or 2 — number is fine.\n- indicating_range_m3: 99999, 99999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 999999 (per DN repeated)\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001 (m³)\n\nConstants → type_level:\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\" (all three DNs) — type_level\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n\nNow the certificate object:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- page_total: 16 (from \"Page 6 of 16 pages\") — present in fragment footers. Include.\n- date_issued: not in fragment. Omit.\n- member_state: \"CZ1\"? The number has CZ1. Member state: Czech Republic — inferable but stamp confirms Czech institute. I could omit member_state or set null. The number fragment \"CZ1\" is the CMI identifier. I'll omit member_state to be safe... Actually stamp \"Český metrologický institut\" strongly implies Czech Republic. But rule: don't fabricate; but this is inference from explicit text. I'll omit member_state — the issuing authority name covers it.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Český metrologický institut\" (from stamp). Include. Address not present. oiml_issuer_id not present — omit.\n\nHmm, is the stamp text sufficient? It's a visible element with text. I'll include the name. The person_responsible etc. not present.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, amendment: null? The certificate number \"R49/2013-A\" indicates R49 edition 2013. Also EUT testing requirements reference \"OIML R 49-2:2013, 8.1.8\". So recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\"}. accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — present in the type details (accuracy class 1 and 2). Include accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\ntest_reports: not present in fragment. Omit.\nrevision_history: not present. Omit.\nfootnotes: none in fragment. Omit.\n\npage info: \"Verze 20-001\" is a form version — not needed. Pages 6 and 7 of 16.\n\nLet me now also consider the \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" — this is a table where the columns are the three modules (HV, LV, LLV), which is the config axis \"power_supply_module\". Good.\n\nAlso note: the earlier main table had per-DN columns spanning 2 sub-columns each (the 6 columns). The Length row on page 7 has only 3 values → per DN. All 200 → type_level.\n\nNow assemble JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 16\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\"name\": \"Český metrologický institut\"}\n```\n\nmanufacturers:\n```json\n[{\"name\": \"Hemina SPA\", \"address_lines\": [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana\", \"350 44 Italy\"]}]\n```\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\"],\n  \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter ... nominal diameters DN50, DN65, DN80 ...\"\n}\n```\ndescription: maybe \"Electromagnetic water meter, model MS2500/MV110, nominal diameters 50, 65 and 80 mm, power supply modules HV, LV, LLV.\" I can include a brief description. Or omit. I'll include a short one summarizing.\n\nmodule_designation: The schema expects \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Here the cert calls the meter \"the module\" with designations MS2500/MV110 HV/LV/LLV. I'll set null? Or omit. Hmm. I'll omit since ambiguous. Actually — maybe include as the measuring principle is \"Electromagnetic water meter\" not a module. Omit.\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"MS2500 / MV110\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN50 Q3/Q1=250\", \"label\": \"DN 50,