ature_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"liner_material\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"liner PTFE\", \"value\": \"T90, T50, T30\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"liner Rilsan (from DN50)\", \"value\": \"T50, T30\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"liner Ebonite (from DN200)\", \"value\": \"T50, T30\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"liner polypropylene\", \"value\": \"T50, T30\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nThe values here are class strings, fine. Note \"For liner Rilsan - T50; T30 from DN50\" — the \"from DN50\" applies to the liner applicability. I'll phrase condition \"liner Rilsan (from DN50)\".\n\n2. maximum_admissible_temperature:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_admissible_temperature\",\n  \"axis\": \"liner_material\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"liner PTFE\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"liner polypropylene, Rilsan, Ebonite\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n3. MPE lower:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone\",\n  \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Class 1\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Class 2\", \"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm — should I use {min:-3,max:3} or \"±3\"? Let me reconsider once more. The strict rule: \"Ranges. 0-9.999 ... become {min,max}\". \"±3%\" isn't literally a range but semantically is. I think {min:-3, max:3} is the best structured representation. OK going with it.\n\n4. MPE upper:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrate_zone\",\n  \"axis\": \"water_temperature and accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C, Class 1\", \"value\": {\"min\": -1, \"max\": 1}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C, Class 2\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"water temperature >